#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_fourier

#% text
# `inverse_fourier(list)` gives the inverse discrete Fourier transform of a list of numbers; `inverse_fourier(array)` transforms along every level.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The inverse transform of `v` of length `n` is `u[r] = 1/n^((1 + a)/2) sum(v[s] exp(-2 pi im b (r - 1)(s - 1)/n), s = 1..n)` with `fourier_parameters: [a, b]` (default `[0, 1]`), so that it undoes `fourier` with the same parameters.

#% item
# The result is numeric, at the precision of the data.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse_fourier([1, 2, 3, 4])
#% output
#= [5.0 + 0.0im, -1.0 + 1.0im, -1.0 + 0.0im, -1.0 - 1.0im]

#% cell
chop(inverse_fourier(fourier([1, 1, 0, 0, 0])))
#% output
#= [1.0000000000000002, 1.0000000000000002, 0, 0, 0]

#% cell
chop(inverse_fourier(fourier([1, 2, 3], fourier_parameters: [1, -1]), fourier_parameters: [1, -1]))
#% output
#= [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `fourier`, `inverse_fourier_transform`, `chop`
